    "media:imagetext": "N.Y. March 6th\n1958\nDear Girls.\nI came up here for a meting of PAA on the 4th and am staying\nover for a meeting of GPU on the 10th - meanwhile engaging in that pleasant\ntask which Uncle Sam reserves for his nephews of making out my income tax.\nIn my odd moments will try to give you a brief run down on our\nrecent trip around So. America.\nThe Director's flight left I.Y. on the\n7th of Feb. with the first stop Havana where Ma and I joined them. We left\nthere on the 9th and after a four hour flight arrived at Curacoa in the\nDutch B West Indies.\nThere PAA built a most attractive hotel on the founda-\ntion of an old fort which guarded the entrance to the harbor. It was sunday\nbut they kept the stores open to the delight of the women and dismay of the\nmen on the trip. On the 10th we made a 72 hour flight across Columbia and a\npart of Venzuela to Lima, Peru.\nThe next morning some of our party flew up\nto Cuzco in a Panagra DO3.\nWe had to tly at 20,000 feet to cross the Andes\nand of course in an unpressurized plane we had to have oxygen.\nThis was\nsupplied through outlets at each seat and we all wore masks - looking for all\nthe world like a lot of horses at chow time. Some of the party did not go\nbecause they were worried about the 11,000 feet altitude at Cuzco. To tell\nthe truth I was too but didn't say anything about it.\nBall when he took delivery of his first airplane he told the pilot to climb\nto 10,000 feet.\nThe pilot objected because they had to make El Paso before\ndark but Mr. Ball insisted. The pilot climbed up to 10,000 and coasted on in\nto El Paso.\nThere he asked again why the old man insisted and Mr. Ball said\n\"I dont mind telling you now.\nThe doctor said it I went up to 7,500 teet\nthat I would drop dead so I just spotted him 2,500 feet to prove that he did\nnot know what the H he was talking about.\" We loved Cuzco and neither of\nus felt the altitude at all. Of course the thing that interested me most was\nthe stone walls that the old Incas built.\nSome of the stones weigh 20 or\n30,000 Ibs. were moved as much as a mile and are so close together that you\ncant put a knife blade between them at any point of the 7 foot joint.\nThis\nthey did without steel chisels - no one knows just how. We stayed overnight\nand the next morning flew back to Lima. On the 13th we flew 7 hours along\nthe coast of Peru and Chili with the unending blue Pacific on our right and\nthe snow capped Andes on the left. It was a magnificent flight. we spent\nthat night and the next in Santiago and on the 15th lew over the nigest\nmountain in this hemisphere and then cireled down in a valley to 15,000 feet\nwhere we had a good look at the Christ of the Andes. After about three hours\nwe landed at Bariloche in the Chilean Lake country. Actually we were just\nover the Chilean border in Argentina some 450 miles south and west of B.A.\nThe lake was something like Lake George - the water as blue - but the mount-\nains all around were rougher, higher and mostly snow capped. We were there\nof course in their summer.\nAt the little hotel where we stayed Mr. Trippe\nhad us all in a little room off the bar for drinks before dinner.\nSomeone\ntapped me on the shoulder and when I turned around there was wes shapleigh.\nThe Ss, Danforths, Stuarts and Whitesides all old friends from St. Louis\nwere on a trip around South America.\nIt was as though you'd run into an old\nfriend at Lake Pharoah.. Frank Gledhill and I got up early the next morning\nand went fishing.\nWe were only out about an hour but he caught an \u0026 lb.\nrainbow trout. we had lunch at a private home on the edge of the lake which",
